<urn:beads-cache:finding:FTQ-009> triage:id "FTQ-009" ; triage:sprint "c-1" ; triage:severity "important" ; triage:location "crates/bd-cache-store/src/schedule.rs:833" ; triage:source "qa-4 /root/c1_flaky4 fenced JSON at95def5f3" ; triage:status "closed" ; triage:record "{\"evidence\": \"schedule.rs:828-834 receives for 500ms, drops the held mutex and calls task.join before checking entered/bounded; :894-900 repeats this after ROLLBACK; :984-994 receives for 500ms, requests interrupt on timeout, then unconditionally joins. None supervises the fixture or bounds thread completion. RustQA affected-tests log:22-24 reports these tests pass, which does not exercise stalled cleanup.\", \"file\": \"crates/bd-cache-store/src/schedule.rs\", \"id\": \"FTQ-009\", \"issue\": \"All three new synchronous shutdown regressions receive a bounded completion result, but then unconditionally join the tested thread before examining that result. A worker read/write that stays stuck after cancellation and external-lock release prevents the timeout assertion from ever reporting failure. The running-SQL variant requests SQLite interruption on timeout but still has no enforceable lifetime if the thread is stalled outside the interruptible statement. Thus these new fixtures repeat the previously corrected FTQ-001 failure-path design in a different module.\", \"line\": 833, \"mechanism\": \"unbounded_wait\", \"recommendation\": \"Place the complete synchronous fixture in an exactly filtered owned child with a hard supervisor deadline, kill/reap cleanup and assertion-unwind cleanup, as the existing health mutex fixture already does. Keep the actual contention probes and existing 30ms cancellation / 500ms observation bounds; do not widen them or detach timed-out threads.\", \"severity\": \"important\", \"test\": \"shutdown_interrupts_an_already_contended_worker_read_only; shutdown_interrupts_busy_persistence_and_restores_the_shared_session; shutdown_interrupts_a_running_sql_statement_and_restores_progress\"}" .

